<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net9.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <RootNamespace>Ten21.Business</RootNamespace>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Ten21.Domain\Ten21.Domain.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Ten21.Application\Ten21.Application.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Ten21.Infrastructure\Ten21.Infrastructure.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The actual business layer: one concrete service class per domain area (ChargeService,
    etc), each depending on a concrete repository class (ChargeRepository, etc) and whatever
    Application interfaces it genuinely needs (IInputSanitizer has a real second
    implementation concern worth abstracting; a plain data-access wrapper around one shared
    Ten21DbContext does not). No interface per class here; see each file's own comment for
    why an interface isn't buying anything for a single-implementation seam nothing else
    swaps out. Everything is still wired through DI (DependencyInjection.cs), never new'd up
    by a caller.

    Referencing Infrastructure directly (for Ten21DbContext) is why this can't be part of
    Ten21.Application: Infrastructure already depends on Application (to implement its
    interfaces), so Application depending back on Infrastructure would be circular. This
    project sits after both in the chain instead: Domain, then Application, then
    Infrastructure, then Business, then Api.
  -->

</Project>
